Routes are defined in top-level files in routes/ and each file exports a Router instance. The filename becomes the route prefix. Example:
import { Router } from "@/core/http/router.ts";

export default new Router("/api", function routerName(router) {
  router.get("/", function handlerName() {
    return () => Response.json({ message: "Hello" });
  });

  router.get("/users/:id", function getUser() {
    return (req) => Response.json({ id: "123" });
  });
}).group("GroupName");

Example validated route:
router.post("/users", function createUser() {
  const $body = z.object({ name: z.string().min(1), email: z.string().email() });
  const $return = z.object({ id: z.string(), name: z.string() });
  return {
    shape: () => ({ body: $body, return: $return }),
    handler: async (req) => {
      const body = $body.parse(await bodyAsJson(req));
      return Response.json({ id: "123", name: body.name } satisfies z.output<typeof $return>);
    },
  };
});

Path parameter examples:
router.get("/users/:userId/posts/:postId", function getPost() { ... });
router.get("/files{/*filepath}", function serveFile() { ... });
router.get("/items{/:id}", function getItems() { ... });
Named parameters are available through paramsAsJson(req).

Request utilities are imported from @/core/http/utils.ts. paramsAsJson reads URL parameters, queryAsJson reads query strings, bodyAsJson parses JSON or form-urlencoded bodies, parseBasicAuth parses the Basic authorization header, and serveAssets serves static files. Example:
const params = paramsAsJson<{ id: string }>(req);
const query = queryAsJson(req);
const body = await bodyAsJson(req);
const auth = parseBasicAuth(req);

Hook example:
import type { THook } from "@/core/http/hooks.ts";

export default {
  priority: 100,
  pre: async ({ req, scope, name }) => {
    const token = req.headers.get("Authorization");
    if (!token) return new Response("Unauthorized", { status: 401 });
  },
  post: async ({ req, res, scope, name }) => {
    // Runs after the handler and may replace the response.
  },
} satisfies THook;

Logging hook example:
import type { THook } from "@/core/http/hooks.ts";
import { Logger } from "@/core/utils/logger.ts";

export default {
  priority: -100,
  post: async ({ req, res, name }) => {
    Logger.info(`[${req.method}] ${req.url} → ${res.status} (${name})`);
  },
} satisfies THook;

Use the Env utility instead of reading Deno.env directly:
import { Env, EnvType } from "@/core/utils/env.ts";
if (Env.is(EnvType.DEVELOPMENT)) { ... }
const dbUrl = await Env.get("DATABASE_URL");
const debug = await Env.enabled("DEBUG");
const port = await Env.number("PORT");
const hosts = await Env.list("ALLOWED_HOSTS");
const syncValue = Env.getSync("DATABASE_URL");
const optional = await Env.get("OPTIONAL_VAR", true);

Thunder does not automatically create database indexes at startup because it is serverless-first. Create scripts under scripts/ and run them manually. Example:
import { userModel } from "@/schemas/user.ts";
async function createIndexes() {
  await userModel.createIndex({ email: 1 }, { unique: true });
  await userModel.createIndex({ createdAt: -1 });
  console.log("✓ Indexes created successfully");
}
await createIndexes();
Run with deno run -A scripts/createIndexes.ts.

A MongoDB schema uses Zod and the shared mongodb connection:
import z from "zod";
import { mongodb } from "@/database.ts";

const $userInput = z.object({ name: z.string(), email: z.string().email() });
const $user = $userInput.extend({
  _id: $objectId.optional(),
  createdAt: z.date().default(() => new Date()),
});
export const userModel = mongodb.db().collection<z.infer<typeof $user>>("users");

Correct reference schema example:
const $post = z.object({
  title: z.string(),
  user: $objectId.meta({ ref: "users" }),
  tenant: $objectId.meta({ ref: "tenants" }),
  categories: z.array($objectId.meta({ ref: "categories" })),
});
Do not use userId, tenantId, or categoryIds for these references.

Basic model operations use the MongoDB collection API. Validate documents before insertion, preferably with the custom type-safe strictParse method, which also applies defaults:
await userModel.insertOne($user.strictParse({ name: "John", email: "john@mail.com" }));
await userModel.findOne({ _id: new ObjectId(id) });
await userModel.updateOne({ _id }, { $set: { name: "Jane" } });
await userModel.deleteOne({ _id });

MongoDB transactions use mongodb.withSession:
await mongodb.withSession(session =>
  session.withTransaction(async () => {
    await userModel.insertOne({ ... }, { session });
    await logs.insertOne({ ... }, { session });
  })
);

createCRUD generates REST endpoints from a schema, model, and input schema:
createCRUD({ router, schema: $user, model: userModel, insertSchema: $userInput, updateSchema: $userInput.pick({ name: true }) }, {
  disable: { del: true },
  hooks: {
    beforeCreate: ({ data }) => ({ ...data, createdBy: "system" }),
    afterCreate: ({ data }) => console.log("Created:", data._id),
  },
});

createCRUD supports isolationFields for ownership or multi-tenancy. During create, returned fields are inserted into the document. During update or deletion, returned fields are included in the isolation query. Example:
isolationFields: async (req, action) => {
  if (action === "create") return { orgId: req.headers.get("X-Org-Id"), userId: req.headers.get("X-User-Id") };
  if (["update", "del"].includes(action)) return { orgId: req.headers.get("X-Org-Id"), userId: req.headers.get("X-User-Id") };
  return { orgId: req.headers.get("X-Org-Id") };
}

Always use Zod for input validation. Define schemas during the prepare phase and parse request data before processing:
const $input = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(1).max(100),
  age: z.coerce.number().int().min(0),
  email: z.string().email().optional(),
  tags: z.array(z.string()).default([]),
});
const data = $input.parse(await bodyAsJson(req));

Query parameters arrive as strings, so use Zod coercion for numeric or other typed query values:
const $query = z.object({
  page: z.coerce.number().default(1),
  limit: z.coerce.number().max(100).default(20),
});

Use Logger from @/core/utils/logger.ts for logging. Supported methods include Logger.info, Logger.success, Logger.warn, and Logger.error. Example:
Logger.info("Processing request...");
Logger.success("Operation completed");
Logger.warn("Rate limit approaching");
Logger.error("Failed to connect", error);
Only errors are logged in production mode.

Use cache from @/core/utils/cache.ts to cache function results with a TTL in milliseconds:
const getUser = cache(async (id: string) => {
  return await db.users.findOne({ _id: id });
}, 60_000);
Use Infinity for a permanent cache.

A separate worker can poll a queue and process emails:
async function processEmailQueue() {
  while (true) {
    const email = await emailQueueModel.findOne({ status: "pending" });
    if (!email) {
      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 5000));
      continue;
    }
    try {
      await sendEmail(email);
      await emailQueueModel.updateOne({ _id: email._id }, { $set: { status: "sent", sentAt: new Date() } });
    } catch (error) {
      await emailQueueModel.updateOne({ _id: email._id }, { $set: { status: "failed", error: error.message } });
    }
  }
}

For third-party queues, enqueue work from a route and process it in a separate worker. A BullMQ route can add a job with retries and exponential backoff:
await emailQueue.add("send", data, {
  attempts: 3,
  backoff: { type: "exponential", delay: 2000 },
});
The route returns immediately while the worker handles sendEmail(job.data).

A MongoDB queue can insert a validated pending record from the route:
await emailQueueModel.insertOne($emailQueue.strictParse({
  type: "send-email",
  data: { to, subject, body },
  status: "pending",
  createdAt: new Date(),
}));
A separate worker processes pending records. Do not run the worker inside the serverless application.

Static files can be served with serveAssets and a wildcard route:
import { paramsAsJson, serveAssets } from "@/core/http/utils.ts";
import { fromFileUrl } from "@std/path/from-file-url";

router.get("{/*path}", function staticFiles() {
  return (req) => serveAssets(
    req,
    fromFileUrl(import.meta.resolve("../public/www")),
    paramsAsJson<{ path?: string[] }>(req).path?.join("/"),
  );
});

Errors are automatically handled. ZodError becomes a 400 response with validation details, a thrown Response is returned as-is, and a regular Error becomes a 500 response with its message. Stack traces are exposed only in development. Custom responses can be thrown:
if (!authorized) {
  throw Response.forbidden("Access denied");
}
throw new Error("Something went wrong");

Lifecycle example:
export const syncDBIndexes = async () => {
  await Promise.allSettled([
    userModel.createIndex({ email: 1 }, { name: "email", unique: true, background: true }),
    orderModel.createIndex({ createdAt: -1 }, { name: "createdAt", background: true }),
  ]);
};
if (import.meta.main) {
  await syncDBIndexes();
  Deno.exit();
}

A minimal plugin route can validate input and expose a semantic endpoint:
export default new Router("/email", function email(router) {
  router.post("/send", function sendEmail() {
    const $body = z.object({ to: z.string().email(), subject: z.string(), body: z.string() });
    return {
      shape: () => ({ body: $body }),
      handler: async (req) => {
        const data = $body.parse(await bodyAsJson(req));
        return Response.json({ success: true, messageId: "msg_123" });
      },
    };
  });
});

Pagination should validate offset and limit with Zod coercion, query the collection with skip and limit, and fetch the count in parallel:
const $query = z.object({ offset: z.coerce.number().min(0).default(0), limit: z.coerce.number().min(1).max(2000).default(100) });
const { offset, limit } = $query.parse(queryAsJson(req));
const [results, count] = await Promise.all([
  collection.find().skip(offset).limit(limit).toArray(),
  collection.countDocuments(),
]);
return Response.json({ results, count });

File uploads use req.formData(), retrieve the File value, reject missing files with Response.badRequest, read bytes with file.arrayBuffer(), and write them with Deno.writeFile. Example:
const formData = await req.formData();
const file = formData.get("file") as File;
if (!file) return Response.badRequest("No file provided");
const bytes = await file.arrayBuffer();
await Deno.writeFile(`./uploads/${file.name}`, new Uint8Array(bytes));
return Response.json({ filename: file.name });

Template rendering utilities accept template content as a string, not a file path. ejsRender is asynchronous and handlebarsRender is synchronous:
const html = await ejsRender("<h1>Hello <%= name %></h1>", { name: "Alice" });
const greeting = handlebarsRender("Hello {{name}}", { name: "Bob" });
const tpl = await Deno.readTextFile("templates/welcome.ejs");
const out = await ejsRender(tpl, { name: "Alice", appName: "Thunder App" });

Hashing example:
const hex = await createHash(SupportedHashAlg.SHA_256, "data");
const b64 = await createHashBase64(SupportedHashAlg.SHA_256, "data");
const hexSync = createHashSync(SupportedHashAlg.SHA_256, "data");
const isMatch = (await createHash(SupportedHashAlg.SHA_256, input)) === stored;

parseQueryParams parses a query string into a nested object through qs. It accepts a string rather than a Request. Route handlers should generally prefer queryAsJson(req), which wraps it:
const params = parseQueryParams("?page=1&filter[status]=active");
// { page: "1", filter: { status: "active" } }

paginated repeatedly invokes a callback with offset and limit until the callback returns fewer items than the limit. It is useful for processing large collections in batches:
await paginated(100, async (offset, limit) => {
  const batch = await collection.find().skip(offset).limit(limit).toArray();
  for (const doc of batch) {
    // process each document
  }
  return batch.length;
});
